.TH AGENTLOG 1 "2026-11-11" "agentlog 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
agentlog \- read and search Claude Code, Codex and pi session logs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agentlog
.I command
.RI [ ref ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agentlog
reads the session transcripts of Claude Code, Codex and pi through one interface.
Its purpose is handoff: when an agent stops mid\-task on a rate limit or a
crash, the work is stranded in a transcript no other agent can read.
.B agentlog resume
turns that transcript into a brief another agent can act on, carrying the
user's instructions verbatim, the files touched, the commands run, and how the
session ended.
.PP
The brief is a record of what was
.I attempted,
not proof that it landed. A resuming agent should re\-verify state on disk.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
Recent sessions, newest first, with the first real prompt of each. Harness
preamble (AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, system reminders, slash\-command echoes) is
stripped so the column shows what the human actually asked.
.TP
.B status
Which recent sessions are rate limited. Checks the 5 most recent by default.
See
.B RATE LIMIT DETECTION
below.
.TP
.BR resume " [" \fIref\fR "]"
Print a handoff brief for a session. Sections: the task (every user
instruction, in order), files created or modified, files read, the last 25
commands run, the final assistant turns, and a NEXT block.
.TP
.BR show " [" \fIref\fR "]"
Full readable transcript. Tool bodies, thinking, and system messages are
omitted unless requested.
.TP
.BI search " regex"
Case\-insensitive regex search across sessions, printing the matching session
refs with context. Fast: a 200\-session scan takes about 0.2s.
.SH SESSION REFS
A
.I ref
is any of:
.TP
.B latest
The newest session. The caller's own live session is excluded, so this is safe
to run from inside an agent.
.TP
.BR prev ", " latest~\fIN\fR
The session before the newest, or N back.
.TP
.I id
A full session id, or any unambiguous prefix of one. An ambiguous prefix lists
the matches and exits 2.
.TP
.IB source : id
Restrict to one source, as in
.BR codex:01a08 .
.I source
alone also works, as in
.BR codex:latest .
.TP
.I path
A path to a log file, read directly.
.SH OPTIONS
These apply to every command and must follow the command name.
.TP
.BI \-\-source " name"
.BR claude ", " codex ", " pi ", or " all
(default). Comma\-separated lists are accepted.
.TP
.BI \-\-project " path"
Only sessions whose working directory is under
.I path
(default
.BR /root ).
Use
.B any
for no filter. This is a prefix match for both sources, so
.B /root
includes
.BR /root/prediction-market-trading .
.TP
.BI \-\-chars " n"
Maximum characters per message block (default 4000). 0 disables truncation.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " file"
Write to
.I file
instead of stdout, creating parent directories as needed.
.PP
Command\-specific options:
.TP
.BR list " " \-\-limit " \fIn\fR, " \-\-since\-hours " \fIn\fR, " \-\-json
.TP
.BR list ", " search " " \-\-since " \fIdate\fR, " \-\-until " \fIdate\fR
Only sessions updated in the window. A
.I date
is
.I YYYY\-MM\-DD
or
.IR "YYYY\-MM\-DD HH:MM" ,
in local time. A date given to
.B \-\-until
covers its whole day. Bad values are usage errors. These combine with
.BR \-\-since\-hours .
.TP
.BR status " " \-n ", " \-\-limit " \fIn\fR, " \-\-json
.TP
.BR resume " " \-\-tail " \fIn\fR"
Assistant turns of closing context (default 3; 0 for none).
.TP
.BR resume " " \-\-max\-turns " \fIn\fR, " \-\-all\-turns
Cap on user turns included (default 25).
.TP
.BR resume " " \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation described under
.BR "RATE LIMIT DETECTION" .
.TP
.BR show " " \-\-tools ", " \-\-thinking ", " \-\-system
Include tool call bodies and results, reasoning, and system messages.
.TP
.BR search " " \-\-limit " \fIn\fR, " \-\-scan " \fIn\fR, " \-\-per\-session " \fIn\fR, " \-\-since\-hours " \fIn\fR, " \-\-case ", " \-\-tools
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH RATE LIMIT DETECTION
Detection is structural. An agent that
.I mentions
a rate limit in prose is not an agent
.I stopped
by one, and grepping text for the phrase produces false positives.
.TP
.B codex
Ground truth is an
.B event_msg/task_complete
record carrying
.BR error.codex_error_info " = " usage_limit_exceeded .
Every
.B token_count
record also carries a
.B rate_limits
block with
.B used_percent
and
.B resets_at
for a 5\-hour primary window and a weekly secondary window, which is where the
usage percentages come from.
.TP
.B claude
Claude Code marks a terminal API failure with
.B isApiErrorMessage
on the assistant record. The error only counts when no reply landed after it,
so a session that hit a 529 and kept working reads
.BR ok .
.PP
Three states are reported.
.B RATE LIMITED
means blocked now, with a countdown to the reset.
.B was limited
means the window has already reopened, so the original agent can likely just
continue and a handoff may be wasted work; in this case
.B resume
prints the notice and asks before producing a brief. The prompt appears only on
a terminal, so a non\-interactive caller is warned and proceeds rather than
hanging.
.B ERROR
is a terminal failure that is not a limit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<uuid>.jsonl
Claude Code transcripts. The directory name is a lossy slug; the working
directory is read from the records themselves.
.TP
.I ~/.codex/sessions/YYYY/MM/DD/rollout-*.jsonl
Codex rollouts.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Check whether anything is blocked before starting work:
.PP
.RS
.EX
agentlog status
.EE
.RE
.PP
Hand a stranded task to another agent:
.PP
.RS
.EX
agentlog resume codex:01a0814e \-\-out /tmp/handoff.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
From inside an agent, pick up whatever died before it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
agentlog resume prev
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find where a topic was last discussed, across all agents and projects:
.PP
.RS
.EX
agentlog search "published_roster" \-\-project any
.EE
.RE
.PP
Machine\-readable status for a script:
.PP
.RS
.EX
agentlog status \-n 10 \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
No session matched the ref, or bad arguments.
.TP
.B 2
Ambiguous ref; the candidates are listed on stderr.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Codex builds a small number of shell commands as arrays or computed values
inside the JavaScript snippet rather than as a literal string. Measured over
12,522 exec calls, 1.7% are not recovered and so do not appear in the
COMMANDS RUN section; extracting them would require evaluating the snippet.
.PP
Hermes is not supported. It records no limit or error state anywhere, so its
sessions could not be reported on honestly and the source was removed rather
than shipped half\-working.
.PP
Colour is emitted only to a terminal, so piped output is clean for another
agent to consume.
.SH SEE ALSO
The source is a single self\-contained Python script at
.IR /root/agentlog/agentlog ,
tracked in git.
